<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Humanitarian partnership principles"><div><strong>Safe water first</strong><span>No hydration product can compensate for unsafe preparation water or weak sanitation systems.</span></div><div><strong>Right formulation</strong><span>Therapeutic diarrhoeal dehydration requires appropriately formulated ORS, not automatic substitution with a consumer hydration product.</span></div><div><strong>Local implementation</strong><span>Partners, logistics, training, monitoring and community context determine whether an access project works.</span></div></div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature"><div><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Implementation before branding</p><h2>Hydration access is a public-health system problem</h2><p>In humanitarian settings, the product itself is only one part of the intervention. Safe water, sanitation, storage, preparation accuracy, local supply, health guidance, trust and continuity all matter. Best Hydrate therefore approaches humanitarian work as a partnership and implementation question rather than assuming that shipping a consumer product is enough.</p><p>Where therapeutic oral rehydration is required, established WHO/UNICEF ORS standards and local clinical or public-health guidance should control the intervention.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__split"><div class="bhd-page__split-media"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/6646918/pexels-photo-6646918.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Community volunteers organizing donated supplies for distribution" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><div class="bhd-page__split-copy"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Where collaboration can help</p><h2>Design the project around the local problem</h2><ul><li>Needs assessment and hydration-use context</li><li>Safe-water and preparation requirements</li><li>Product or formulation suitability</li><li>Local distribution and storage</li><li>Training and communication materials</li><li>Monitoring, feedback and implementation learning</li></ul><p>A useful partnership begins by deciding which of those problems is actually limiting access.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Potential collaboration models</p><h2>Four ways a project could be structured</h2><div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4"><article><h3>Pilot access project</h3><p>Test a defined use case at small scale with local oversight and measurable implementation outcomes.</p></article><article><h3>Research partnership</h3><p>Study acceptability, preparation, adherence, logistics or other implementation questions under an appropriate protocol.</p></article><article><h3>Distribution partnership</h3><p>Explore local or regional supply where product fit, regulation and infrastructure make sense.</p></article><article><h3>Program design</h3><p>Work with health, NGO, community or institutional partners to integrate hydration into a broader program.</p></article></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid"><article class="bhd-page__prose"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">What we will not blur</p><h2>Consumer hydration and therapeutic ORS are not automatically the same product category</h2><p>Best Hydrate's current consumer formula and WHO reduced-osmolarity ORS should not be treated as interchangeable merely because both involve glucose and electrolytes. Therapeutic ORS has defined composition and use standards, particularly for diarrhoeal dehydration.</p><p>Humanitarian collaboration can include product development, research, implementation or access work, but the formulation and regulatory pathway must match the intended use.</p></article><aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Non-negotiable</span><h3>Use the standard that matches the health problem.</h3><p>Mission-driven work still requires formulation, water safety and clinical boundaries.</p></aside></section>

<section class="bhd-page__brand-context"><div><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Humanitarian inquiry</p><h2>Bring the local context first</h2><p>Tell us the location, population, existing water and health infrastructure, intended use, local partners and the problem you are trying to solve. That information matters more than starting with a predetermined product.</p></div><div class="bhd-page__brand-actions"><a class="bhd-page__button" href="/pages/contact">Discuss a Project</a><a class="bhd-page__button bhd-page__button--secondary" href="/pages/eco-humanitarian">Eco-Humanitarian</a></div></section>

